import argparse,copy,datetime,json,pathlib,shutil
SPEC = {'run': 'numerical-optimization-dynamics-2026-10-16', 'manifest_path': 'research/numerical-optimization-dynamics-2026-10-16-batch-7.pages.json', 'contracts_path': 'research/numerical-optimization-dynamics-2026-10-16-batch-7.proof-contracts.json', 'source_guards': {'items/thm-forward-euler-one-step-consistency-and-local-error.md': '---\nid: thm-forward-euler-one-step-consistency-and-local-error\nkind: theorem\ntitle: "Forward Euler has uniform local order one and a one-step Lipschitz bound"\nstatus: draft\norigin: pipeline\npipeline_run: numerical-optimization-dynamics-2026-10-16\ndeps:\n  - def-one-step-ode-method-local-defect-order-and-stability\n  - def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space\n  - def-euclidean-inner-product\n  - thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm\n  - def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution\n  - lem-time-along-ode-solution-derivative-bound\n  - prop-first-order-ivp-is-equivalent-to-a-volterra-integral-equation\n  - cor-mean-value-theorem\n  - thm-continuous-partial-derivatives-imply-total-differentiability\n  - thm-total-differentiability-gives-a-local-linear-bound-and-continuity\n  - thm-mean-value-inequality-for-total-derivatives\n  - thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution\njustified_by: []\naliases: []\nproof_strategy: direct\nprovenance:\n  statement: ai-altered\n  proof: ai-altered\nverification:\n  precheck: pass\nsources:\n  references:\n    - title: "Ernst Hairer, Syvert P. Nørsett, and Gerhard Wanner, Solving Ordinary Differential Equations I: Nonstiff Problems, 2nd ed., Chapter III, Sections III.2–III.3"\n      url: "https://www.unige.ch/~hairer/poly/chap3.pdf"\n    - title: "MIT OpenCourseWare, 18.330 Introduction to Numerical Analysis (Spring 2012), Chapter 5, Sections 5.2.1–5.2.2"\n      url: "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-330-introduction-to-numerical-analysis-spring-2012/a9d2bd9be098f0ada172af40379a17cc_MIT18_330S12_Chapter5.pdf"\n    - title: "Tobin A. Driscoll and Richard J. Braun, Fundamentals of Numerical Computation, Julia Edition, Sections 6.2.1–6.2.2"\n      url: "https://fncbook.com/euler/"\ndependency_level: 1\n---\n\n## Statement\n\nLet $d\\ge1$, $T>0$, and let $V\\subseteq\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R^d$ be an open time-state domain whose fibers $V_t:=\\{z:(t,z)\\in V\\}$ are open and convex. Let $K\\subset V$ be compact. Let $f:V\\to\\mathbb R^d$ have continuous first partial derivatives, and suppose $y$ is a solution of $y\'=f(t,y)$, $y(0)=y_0$, on an open interval containing $[0,T]$, with graph on $[0,T]$ contained in $K$. For each local defect under consideration, require the Euler input and output time-state pairs to lie in $K$; the exact solution segment already lies in $K$.\n\nFor $1\\le i,j\\le d$, assume finite constants $F_j,M_{t,i},M_{ij},L\\ge0$ satisfy throughout $V$\n\n$$|f_j(t,z)|\\le F_j,\\qquad |\\partial_t f_i(t,z)|\\le M_{t,i},\\qquad |\\partial_{z_j}f_i(t,z)|\\le M_{ij},\\qquad \\|D_yf(t,z)w\\|_2\\le L\\|w\\|_2\\quad(w\\in\\mathbb R^d).$$\n\nHere $D_yf$ is the matrix with entries $\\partial_{z_j}f_i$, and the last inequality defines the operator bound used below. The forward Euler map is $\\Psi_h(t,v)=v+h f(t,v)$. Then, with $B_i:=M_{t,i}+\\sum_{j=1}^dM_{ij}F_j$, for every $t\\in[0,T)$ and $h>0$ with $t+h\\le T$ whose Euler input and output pairs lie in $K$, its local defect satisfies\n\n$$\\|\\delta_h(t;y)\\|_2\\le \\frac{h^2}{2}\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^d B_i^2\\right)^{1/2},$$\n\nso it has uniform local order one on $K$ along $y|_{[0,T]}$, with $h_0=T$. For every $t\\in[0,T]$, every admitted $h>0$, and every $u,v\\in V_t$,\n\n$$\\|\\Psi_h(t,u)-\\Psi_h(t,v)\\|_2\\le(1+Lh)\\|u-v\\|_2.$$\n\nThis is a local defect and one-step stability result. It does not assert a global error estimate or an order for any other named method.\n\n## Facts & Assumptions\n\n**Given:** The stated open ODE domain with open convex state fibers, continuous first partial derivatives and bounds, the solution through $(0,y_0)$ on an open interval containing $[0,T]$, the compact set $K$ containing its graph and the Euler time-state pairs under consideration, and the definition of uniform local order.\n\n[F1] [[def-one-step-ode-method-local-defect-order-and-stability]]: $\\delta_h(t;y)=\\Psi_h(t,y(t))-y(t+h)$; uniform local order one means a uniform $O(h^2)$ defect; one-step Lipschitz stability is the displayed $1+Lh$ bound.\n\n[F2] [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]: an IVP has open time-state domain, and a solution is differentiable on an interval, has graph in the domain, and satisfies $y\'=f(t,y)$ and its initial value.\n\n[F3] [[thm-continuous-partial-derivatives-imply-total-differentiability]]: continuous first partial derivatives give a total derivative whose matrix is the Jacobian of those partials.\n\n[F4] [[thm-total-differentiability-gives-a-local-linear-bound-and-continuity]]: total differentiability implies continuity at each point.\n\n[F5] [[def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space]]: a convex set contains the segment between each pair of its points; each open convex state fiber has this property.\n\n[F6] [[thm-mean-value-inequality-for-total-derivatives]]: on an open convex Euclidean set, a uniform derivative bound $M$ implies $\\|g(u)-g(v)\\|_2\\le M\\|u-v\\|_2$.\n\n[F7] [[thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution]]: a continuous vector field locally Lipschitz in the state variable has a unique maximal IVP solution. The given derivative bound and [F3]–[F6] provide that local Lipschitz condition on each state fiber.\n\n[F8] [[lem-time-along-ode-solution-derivative-bound]]: along an exact solution, $g_i(s)=f_i(s,y(s))$ has derivative bounded by $B_i=M_{t,i}+\\sum_{k=1}^dM_{ik}F_k$.\n\n[F9] [[prop-first-order-ivp-is-equivalent-to-a-volterra-integral-equation]]: for any initial time $t$ on the solution interval, the solution satisfies $y(t+h)=y(t)+\\int_t^{t+h}f(s,y(s))\\,ds$.\n\n[F10] [[cor-mean-value-theorem]]: if a real function is continuous on a closed interval and differentiable in its interior, its endpoint difference is its derivative at an interior point times the interval length.\n\n[F11] [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]: for $x\\in\\mathbb R^d$, $\\|x\\|_2^2=\\sum_{i=1}^d x_i^2$.\n\n[F12] [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]]: for $x,z\\in\\mathbb R^d$, the Euclidean norm satisfies $\\|x+z\\|_2\\le\\|x\\|_2+\\|z\\|_2$.\n\n## Proof\n\n1.1 By [F3]–[F4], $f$ is totally differentiable and continuous on $V$, with state derivative matrix $D_yf$. On each open convex fiber $V_t$, the derivative bound and [F5]–[F6] make $f(t,\\cdot)$ $L$-Lipschitz. Thus the hypotheses of [F7] hold and the IVP has a unique maximal solution; the given solution agrees with it throughout $[0,T]$. [given, F2, F3, F4, F5, F6, F7]\n\n1.2 Fix any $t\\in[0,T)$ and $h>0$ with $t+h\\le T$ satisfying the stated compact-set condition, and fix a component $i$. Define $g_i(s):=f_i(t+s,y(t+s))$ for $0\\le s\\le h$. The exact path segment lies in $K\\subset V$, so [F8] gives $|g_i\'(s)|\\le B_i$ on $(0,h)$. [given, F2, F3, F8]\n\n2.1 For each $s\\in(0,h]$, [F10] applied to $g_i$ on $[0,s]$ yields $|g_i(s)-g_i(0)|\\le B_i s$; at $s=0$ the same inequality is equality. [step 1.2, F10]\n\n3.1 Apply [F9] to the IVP restarted at $(t,y(t))$; then $y_i(t+h)-y_i(t)=\\int_0^h g_i(s)\\,ds$. Euler\'s output from the exact state is $y_i(t)+hg_i(0)$, so $\\delta_{h,i}(t;y)=\\int_0^h(g_i(0)-g_i(s))\\,ds$. Using [step 2.1] gives $|\\delta_{h,i}(t;y)|\\le\\int_0^h B_i s\\,ds=B_i h^2/2$. Squaring the component bounds, summing, and using [F11] gives the asserted Euclidean norm estimate. Its constant is independent of $t$ and $h$, proving uniform local order one under [F1]. [F1, F9, F11, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 For any admitted $t\\in[0,T]$, $h>0$, and $u,v\\in V_t$, [F3] identifies the derivative of $z\\mapsto f(t,z)$ with $D_yf(t,z)$; [F5]–[F6] give $\\|f(t,u)-f(t,v)\\|_2\\le L\\|u-v\\|_2$. Therefore [F12] gives $\\|\\Psi_h(t,u)-\\Psi_h(t,v)\\|_2\\le\\|u-v\\|_2+h\\|f(t,u)-f(t,v)\\|_2\\le(1+Lh)\\|u-v\\|_2$, which is the one-step stability bound in [F1] for inputs in $K$. [F1, F3, F5, F6, F12, algebra] ∎\n\n## Remarks\n\nThe defect proof is choice-free and uses only the time-along-flow chain rule and the componentwise integral remainder. The Taylor supplier is used by the scalar linear example, not needed to prove this theorem. The global error recurrence in the cited numerical-analysis sources is outside this item.\n', 'items/ex-explicit-midpoint-local-order-drop-at-nonsmooth-time-kink.md': '---\nid: ex-explicit-midpoint-local-order-drop-at-nonsmooth-time-kink\nkind: example\ntitle: "A time kink reduces the explicit midpoint local order"\nstatus: draft\norigin: pipeline\npipeline_run: numerical-optimization-dynamics-2026-10-16\ndeps: [def-one-step-ode-method-local-defect-order-and-stability]\njustified_by: []\naliases: []\nproof_strategy: direct\ngeneration:\n  role: example\nprovenance:\n  statement: ai-generated\n  proof: ai-generated\nverification:\n  precheck: pass\nsources:\n  references:\n    - title: "Ernst Hairer, Syvert P. Nørsett, and Gerhard Wanner, Solving Ordinary Differential Equations I: Nonstiff Problems, 2nd ed., Chapter III, Section III.2"\n      url: "https://www.unige.ch/~hairer/poly/chap3.pdf"\n    - title: "MIT OpenCourseWare, 18.330 Introduction to Numerical Analysis (Spring 2012), Chapter 5"\n      url: "https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-330-introduction-to-numerical-analysis-spring-2012/a9d2bd9be098f0ada172af40379a17cc_MIT18_330S12_Chapter5.pdf"\ndependency_level: 1\n---\n\n## Example\n\nOn the domain $D=\\mathbb R^2$, consider $y\'=|t|$ with exact solution $y(t)=\\tfrac12t|t|$ and the explicit midpoint step\n\n$$M_h(t,z):=z+h f\\left(t+\\tfrac h2,z+\\tfrac h2f(t,z)\\right).$$\n\nFor $0<h\\le1$, use the fixed compact time-state rectangle $K=[-1,1]\\times[-1,1]$. On the step starting at $t_h=-h/2$, the exact solution segment, the method input, midpoint stage, and method output all lie in $K$. This step has local defect $-h^2/4$. As $h\\downarrow0$ these admitted starting times stay in $[-1,1]$ and the defect is $\\Theta(h^2)$, so the uniform local order-two bound $O(h^3)$ fails at the time kink.\n\n## Verification\n\n**Given:** $0<h\\le1$, $f(t,z)=|t|$, $y(t)=\\tfrac12t|t|$, and the displayed explicit midpoint map. Compute its local defect on $[-h/2,h/2]$.\n\n[F1] [[def-one-step-ode-method-local-defect-order-and-stability]]: the local defect is method output from an exact starting value minus the exact endpoint; uniform local order two requires a uniform $O(h^3)$ bound.\n\n1.1 For $t<0$, $y(t)=-t^2/2$ and $y\'(t)=-t=|t|$; for $t>0$, $y(t)=t^2/2$ and $y\'(t)=t=|t|$. At $t=0$, $\\lim_{s\\to0}(y(s)-y(0))/s=\\lim_{s\\to0}|s|/2=0=|0|$. Hence $y$ is the exact solution through $y(0)=0$. [given, algebra]\n\n2.1 At $t_h=-h/2$, the exact value is $y(t_h)=-h^2/8$ and the explicit midpoint stage time is $0$, where $f(0,z)=0$ for every stage value $z$. Thus $M_h(t_h,y(t_h))=y(t_h)=-h^2/8$, while $y(t_h+h)=y(h/2)=h^2/8$. [given, F1, step 1.1, algebra]\n\n3.1 Therefore $\\delta_h(t_h;y)=-h^2/4$. The exact segment has $|t|\\le h/2\\le1/2$ and $|y(t)|\\le1/8$; the input and output states are $-h^2/8$, and the midpoint stage state is $h^2/8$, all in $[-1,1]$. Their time coordinates also lie in $[-1,1]$, so the definition applies on the fixed compact set $K$. If a uniform order-two bound held, $h^2/4\\le Ch^3$ for every $0<h\\le1$, forcing $C\\ge1/(4h)$ as $h\\downarrow0$, which is impossible. [F1, step 2.1, algebra]\n\n4.1 For an arbitrary $t\\in\\mathbb R$ and $h>0$, the method increment from the exact state is $h|t+h/2|$. If $[t,t+h]$ lies on either side of $0$, then $y$ is quadratic there and this increment equals $y(t+h)-y(t)$, so the defect is zero. If $t<0<t+h$, put $r=-t/h\\in(0,1)$. Using $y(t)=-t^2/2$ and $y(t+h)=(t+h)^2/2$ gives $\\delta_h(t;y)=-h^2r^2$ for $0<r\\le1/2$ and $\\delta_h(t;y)=-h^2(1-r)^2$ for $1/2\\le r<1$. Thus every crossing step has $|\\delta_h(t;y)|\\le h^2/4$, and the same bound holds for every step. In particular, it holds uniformly for all admitted starts and steps in $K$; together with step 3.1, the uniform local order is exactly one. [F1, given, step 1.1, algebra] ∎\n\n## Remarks\n\nHairer–Nørsett–Wanner\'s midpoint construction and its order discussion assume sufficient smoothness; this verification does not import a smooth-field order theorem. It directly disproves an $O(h^3)$ local bound at the nonsmooth time kink.\n'}, 'supplier_path': 'items/thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm.md', 'supplier_statement_guard': '\nLet $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ and let $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$, with the Euclidean\ninner product and the Euclidean norm as in [[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. Then:\n\n1. **Cauchy-Schwarz.**\n   $$\\lvert\\langle x,y\\rangle\\rvert \\;\\le\\; \\lVert x\\rVert_2\\,\\lVert y\\rVert_2 ,$$\n   with equality if and only if there is a pair $(\\lambda,\\mu) \\ne (0,0)$ of\n   reals with $\\lambda x_k = \\mu y_k$ for every $k < n$.\n2. **$\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ is a norm on $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$**\n   ([[def-norm-and-normed-space]]), for **every** $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$; the metric\n   it induces is $d_2$ of [[lem-metrics-on-rn]] whenever $n \\ge 1$.\n3. **Parallelogram law.**\n   $$\\lVert x+y\\rVert_2^{2} + \\lVert x-y\\rVert_2^{2} \\;=\\; 2\\lVert x\\rVert_2^{2} + 2\\lVert y\\rVert_2^{2} .$$\n4. **Polarisation.**\n   $$\\langle x,y\\rangle \\;=\\; \\tfrac14\\Bigl(\\lVert x+y\\rVert_2^{2} - \\lVert x-y\\rVert_2^{2}\\Bigr),$$\n   so the inner product is recovered from the norm it induces.\n\n**Clause 1 is a citation, not a new proof.** The inequality and its equality case\nare the published [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-finite]], stated there for two lists of\nreals; all that happens below is that it is read in the vector notation of\n[[def-euclidean-inner-product]]. Re-proving it here would put two proofs of one\nstatement in the library.\n', 'euler_id': 'thm-forward-euler-one-step-consistency-and-local-error', 'midpoint_id': 'ex-explicit-midpoint-local-order-drop-at-nonsmooth-time-kink', 'supplier_id': 'thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm', 'old_manifest_deps': ['def-one-step-ode-method-local-defect-order-and-stability', 'lem-time-along-ode-solution-derivative-bound', 'def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space', 'def-euclidean-inner-product', 'def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution', 'prop-first-order-ivp-is-equivalent-to-a-volterra-integral-equation', 'cor-mean-value-theorem', 'thm-continuous-partial-derivatives-imply-total-differentiability', 'thm-total-differentiability-gives-a-local-linear-bound-and-continuity', 'thm-mean-value-inequality-for-total-derivatives', 'thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution'], 'old_euler_citations': [{'fact': 'F1', 'source': 'def-one-step-ode-method-local-defect-order-and-stability', 'source_section': 'Definition', 'quote': 'local defect** is', 'uses': ['3.1', '4.1'], 'use_explanation': 'This fixes the defect sign convention and the order/stability properties in the conclusion.'}, {'fact': 'F2', 'source': 'def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution', 'source_section': 'Definition', 'quote': 'A **solution on an interval** $I$ is a function $x:I\\to\\mathbb R^n$ such that $t_0\\in I$, $(t,x(t))\\in D$ for every $t\\in I$, $x$ is differentiable on $I$ in the domain-relative sense of [[def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral]], and', 'uses': ['1.1', '1.2'], 'use_explanation': 'The solution definition supplies graph-domain membership and the differential equation.'}, {'fact': 'F3', 'source': 'thm-continuous-partial-derivatives-imply-total-differentiability', 'source_section': 'Statement', 'quote': 'Suppose every partial derivative $\\partial_jf$ exists on a neighbourhood of $a\\in U$ and is continuous at $a$. Then $f$ is totally differentiable at $a$, and $Df(a)$ is the linear map with matrix $Jf(a)$.', 'uses': ['1.1', '1.2', '4.1'], 'use_explanation': 'Continuous partials give the Jacobian as the total derivative on V.'}, {'fact': 'F4', 'source': 'thm-total-differentiability-gives-a-local-linear-bound-and-continuity', 'source_section': 'Statement', 'quote': 'If $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ is totally differentiable at $a\\in U$, then some $C,\\delta>0$ satisfy $\\|f(a+h)-f(a)\\|_2\\le C\\|h\\|_2$ whenever $\\|h\\|_2<\\delta$ and $a+h\\in U$. In particular $f$ is continuous at $a$.', 'uses': ['1.1'], 'use_explanation': 'Total differentiability gives continuity needed for IVP existence and the solution path.'}, {'fact': 'F5', 'source': 'def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space', 'source_section': 'Definition', 'quote': 'A subset $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ is **convex** when, for all $x,y\\in U$ and $t\\in[0,1]$ ([[def-interval]]), the point $(1-t)x+ty$ lies in $U$. Thus the full line segment from $x$ to $y$ remains in $U$.', 'uses': ['1.1', '4.1'], 'use_explanation': 'Convex state fibers keep line segments in the domain for the mean-value bound.'}, {'fact': 'F6', 'source': 'thm-mean-value-inequality-for-total-derivatives', 'source_section': 'Statement', 'quote': 'Let $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be convex and open, and let $f:U\\to\\mathbb R^n$ be totally differentiable at every point. If $M\\ge0$ satisfies $\\|Df(z)v\\|_2\\le M\\|v\\|_2$ for every $z\\in U$ and $v\\in\\mathbb R^m$, then', 'uses': ['1.1', '4.1'], 'use_explanation': 'The derivative operator bound yields the state Lipschitz inequality on each fiber.'}, {'fact': 'F7', 'source': 'thm-existence-and-uniqueness-of-a-maximal-ode-solution', 'source_section': 'Statement', 'quote': 'Every initial value problem satisfying the Picard-Lindelof hypotheses has a unique maximal solution. Its domain is an open interval containing the initial time, and every other solution through the same data is its restriction.', 'uses': ['1.1'], 'use_explanation': 'This supplier gives local existence and uniqueness once continuity and state Lipschitzness are checked.'}, {'fact': 'F8', 'source': 'lem-time-along-ode-solution-derivative-bound', 'source_section': 'Statement', 'quote': 'Define $g_i(s):=f_i(s,y(s))$ and $B_i:=M_{t,i}+\\sum_{j=1}^dM_{ij}F_j$. Then $g_i$ is continuous on $[a,b]$, differentiable on $(a,b)$, and', 'uses': ['1.2'], 'use_explanation': 'The local A-page lemma supplies the exact derivative formula and componentwise bound B_i.'}, {'fact': 'F9', 'source': 'prop-first-order-ivp-is-equivalent-to-a-volterra-integral-equation', 'source_section': 'Statement', 'quote': 'A curve solves the IVP if and only if it satisfies the associated Volterra integral equation. Explicitly, the equation is', 'uses': ['3.1'], 'use_explanation': 'The integral-equation form supplies the exact solution increment on each restarted step.'}, {'fact': 'F10', 'source': 'cor-mean-value-theorem', 'source_section': 'Statement', 'quote': 'Let $a, b \\in \\mathbb{R}$ with $a < b$ and let $f : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be\ncontinuous on $[a,b]$ ([[def-continuity-real]], [[def-interval]]) and\ndifferentiable at every point of $(a,b)$ as a function on $[a,b]$\n([[def-derivative]]). Then there is $c \\in (a,b)$ with', 'uses': ['2.1'], 'use_explanation': 'The real mean value theorem turns the derivative bound into |g_i(s)-g_i(0)|≤B_i s.'}, {'fact': 'F11', 'source': 'def-euclidean-inner-product', 'source_section': 'Definition', 'quote': '**Euclidean norm** of $x$ is', 'uses': ['3.1'], 'use_explanation': 'The Euclidean norm squared is the sum of squared components.'}], 'old_euler_derivation4': {'id': 'derive-4.1', 'step': '4.1', 'claim': 'For any admitted $t\\in[0,T]$, $h>0$, and $u,v\\in V_t$, [F3] identifies the derivative of $z\\mapsto f(t,z)$ with $D_yf(t,z)$; [F5]–[F6] give $\\|f(t,u)-f(t,v)\\|_2\\le L\\|u-v\\|_2$. Therefore the triangle inequality gives $\\|\\Psi_h(t,u)-\\Psi_h(t,v)\\|_2\\le\\|u-v\\|_2+h\\|f(t,u)-f(t,v)\\|_2\\le(1+Lh)\\|u-v\\|_2$, which is the one-step stability bound in [F1] for inputs in $K$.', 'inputs': ['F1', 'F3', 'F5', 'F6', 'Given hypotheses and constructions.']}, 'old_midpoint_citation1': {'fact': 'F1', 'source': 'def-one-step-ode-method-local-defect-order-and-stability', 'source_section': 'Definition', 'quote': 'local defect** is', 'uses': ['2.1', '3.1'], 'use_explanation': 'The definition gives the local defect and the uniform h^3 requirement for order two.'}, 'old_midpoint_derivations': [{'id': 'derive-1.1', 'step': '1.1', 'claim': "For $t<0$, $y(t)=-t^2/2$ and $y'(t)=-t=|t|$; for $t>0$, $y(t)=t^2/2$ and $y'(t)=t=|t|$. At $t=0$, $\\lim_{s\\to0}(y(s)-y(0))/s=\\lim_{s\\to0}|s|/2=0=|0|$. Hence $y$ is the exact solution through $y(0)=0$.", 'inputs': ['Given hypotheses and constructions.']}, {'id': 'derive-2.1', 'step': '2.1', 'claim': 'At $t_h=-h/2$, the exact value is $y(t_h)=-h^2/8$ and the explicit midpoint stage time is $0$, where $f(0,z)=0$ for every stage value $z$. Thus $M_h(t_h,y(t_h))=y(t_h)=-h^2/8$, while $y(t_h+h)=y(h/2)=h^2/8$.', 'inputs': ['Given hypotheses and constructions.', 'F1', 'step 1.1']}, {'id': 'derive-3.1', 'step': '3.1', 'claim': 'Therefore $\\delta_h(t_h;y)=-h^2/4$. The exact segment has $|t|\\le h/2\\le1/2$ and $|y(t)|\\le1/8$; the input and output states are $-h^2/8$, and the midpoint stage state is $h^2/8$, all in $[-1,1]$. Their time coordinates also lie in $[-1,1]$, so the definition applies on the fixed compact set $K$. If a uniform order-two bound held, $h^2/4\\le Ch^3$ for every $0<h\\le1$, forcing $C\\ge1/(4h)$ as $h\\downarrow0$, which is impossible. The exact defect is $O(h^2)$ but not $O(h^3)$, so this nonsmooth field reduces the local order to one.', 'inputs': ['F1', 'step 2.1', 'Given hypotheses and constructions.']}], 'new_euler_citation12': {'fact': 'F12', 'source': 'thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm', 'source_section': 'Statement', 'quote': '2. **$\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ is a norm on $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$**\n   ([[def-norm-and-normed-space]]), for **every** $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$; the metric\n   it induces is $d_2$ of [[lem-metrics-on-rn]] whenever $n \\ge 1$.', 'uses': ['4.1'], 'use_explanation': 'The Euclidean norm triangle inequality bounds the sum of the state difference and h times the vector-field difference.'}, 'new_euler_claim4': 'For any admitted $t\\in[0,T]$, $h>0$, and $u,v\\in V_t$, [F3] identifies the derivative of $z\\mapsto f(t,z)$ with $D_yf(t,z)$; [F5]–[F6] give $\\|f(t,u)-f(t,v)\\|_2\\le L\\|u-v\\|_2$. Therefore [F12] gives $\\|\\Psi_h(t,u)-\\Psi_h(t,v)\\|_2\\le\\|u-v\\|_2+h\\|f(t,u)-f(t,v)\\|_2\\le(1+Lh)\\|u-v\\|_2$, which is the one-step stability bound in [F1] for inputs in $K$.', 'new_midpoint_claim3': 'Therefore $\\delta_h(t_h;y)=-h^2/4$. The exact segment has $|t|\\le h/2\\le1/2$ and $|y(t)|\\le1/8$; the input and output states are $-h^2/8$, and the midpoint stage state is $h^2/8$, all in $[-1,1]$. Their time coordinates also lie in $[-1,1]$, so the definition applies on the fixed compact set $K$. If a uniform order-two bound held, $h^2/4\\le Ch^3$ for every $0<h\\le1$, forcing $C\\ge1/(4h)$ as $h\\downarrow0$, which is impossible.', 'new_midpoint_derivation4': {'id': 'derive-4.1', 'step': '4.1', 'claim': 'For an arbitrary $t\\in\\mathbb R$ and $h>0$, the method increment from the exact state is $h|t+h/2|$. If $[t,t+h]$ lies on either side of $0$, then $y$ is quadratic there and this increment equals $y(t+h)-y(t)$, so the defect is zero. If $t<0<t+h$, put $r=-t/h\\in(0,1)$. Using $y(t)=-t^2/2$ and $y(t+h)=(t+h)^2/2$ gives $\\delta_h(t;y)=-h^2r^2$ for $0<r\\le1/2$ and $\\delta_h(t;y)=-h^2(1-r)^2$ for $1/2\\le r<1$. Thus every crossing step has $|\\delta_h(t;y)|\\le h^2/4$, and the same bound holds for every step. In particular, it holds uniformly for all admitted starts and steps in $K$; together with step 3.1, the uniform local order is exactly one.', 'inputs': ['F1', 'Given hypotheses and constructions.', 'step 1.1']}}

def require(ok, message):
    if not ok: raise SystemExit('REFUSED: '+message)

parser=argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)
parser.add_argument('--apply',action='store_true')
args=parser.parse_args()
root=pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent
for rel, expected in SPEC['source_guards'].items():
    require((root/rel).read_text()==expected, 'source changed: '+rel)
supplier=(root/SPEC['supplier_path']).read_text()
statement=supplier.split('## Statement\n',1)[1].split('\n## ',1)[0]
require(statement==SPEC['supplier_statement_guard'],'supplier Statement changed')
mp=root/SPEC['manifest_path']; cp=root/SPEC['contracts_path']
manifest=json.loads(mp.read_text()); contracts=json.loads(cp.read_text())
ei=next(i for p in manifest for i in p['items'] if i['id']==SPEC['euler_id'])
ec=contracts['contracts'][SPEC['euler_id']];mc=contracts['contracts'][SPEC['midpoint_id']]
e4=next(x for x in ec['derivations'] if x['step']=='4.1')
require(ei['deps']==SPEC['old_manifest_deps'],'old Euler manifest deps differ')
require(ec['citations']==SPEC['old_euler_citations'],'old Euler citation fields differ')
require(e4==SPEC['old_euler_derivation4'],'old Euler derivation4.1 differs')
require(mc['citations'][0]==SPEC['old_midpoint_citation1'],'old midpoint F1 differs')
require(mc['derivations']==SPEC['old_midpoint_derivations'],'old midpoint derivations differ')
require(not any(x.get('fact')=='F12' for x in ec['citations']),'F12 already exists')
require(not any(x.get('step')=='4.1' for x in mc['derivations']),'midpoint4.1 already exists')
# Modify only authorized fields; unrelated fields remain as loaded at application time.
ei['deps'].insert(ei['deps'].index('def-euclidean-inner-product')+1,SPEC['supplier_id'])
ec['citations'].append(copy.deepcopy(SPEC['new_euler_citation12']))
e4['claim']=SPEC['new_euler_claim4'];e4['inputs'].insert(e4['inputs'].index('Given hypotheses and constructions.'),'F12')
mc['citations'][0]['uses'].append('4.1')
next(x for x in mc['derivations'] if x['step']=='3.1')['claim']=SPEC['new_midpoint_claim3']
mc['derivations'].append(copy.deepcopy(SPEC['new_midpoint_derivation4']))
proposal={'guards':'exact two whole source bodies + actual supplier Statement + exact old targeted metadata fields','writes':[SPEC['manifest_path'],SPEC['contracts_path']],'changes':['Euler manifest deps: add published Euclidean norm supplier','Euler contract: append exact F12 citation; replace4.1 claim and add F12 input','Midpoint contract: F1 uses add4.1; replace3.1 claim; append exact4.1 derivation'],'native_history':'untouched','applied':False}
if args.apply:
    stamp=datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).strftime('%Y%m%dT%H%M%S%fZ')
    archive=root/'research'/(SPEC['run']+'-owner-step2-batch7-mirrors-before-'+stamp)
    archive.mkdir()
    for p in [mp,cp]: shutil.copy2(p,archive/p.name)
    for rel in SPEC['source_guards']: shutil.copy2(root/rel,archive/pathlib.Path(rel).name)
    shutil.copy2(root/SPEC['supplier_path'],archive/pathlib.Path(SPEC['supplier_path']).name)
    mp.write_text(json.dumps(manifest,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)+'\n')
    cp.write_text(json.dumps(contracts,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)+'\n')
    proposal.update(applied=True,before_archive=str(archive.relative_to(root)))
    receipt=root/'research'/(SPEC['run']+'-owner-step2-batch7-mirrors-completion-'+stamp+'.json')
    receipt.write_text(json.dumps(proposal,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)+'\n')
    proposal['completion']=str(receipt.relative_to(root))
print(json.dumps(proposal,indent=2,ensure_ascii=False))
